[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm getting a modpost error when build testing for arm32:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Text only\"><pre><span></span><code>ERROR: modpost: drivers/pwm/pwm_th1520.ko: symbol '__aeabi_uldivmod' undefined!\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Looks like a 64 bit division on 32 bit hardware, probably this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">ns_to_cycles</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ns</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rate_hz</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC_U64</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">time</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ns</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">saturating_mul</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rate_hz</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC_U64</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">cycles_to_ns</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cycles</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u32</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rate_hz</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC_U64</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">time</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">from</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cycles</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NSEC_PER_SEC_U64</span><span class=\"p\">).</span><span class=\"n\">div_ceil</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rate_hz</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm not usually building for arm32, so I'm wondering if it is user error on my end.  But I seem to recall that 64 bit division on 32 bit hardware is a no-go, is that correct? Should the driver be gated on <code>CONFIG_64BIT</code>?</p>",
        "id": 626825599,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790324506
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We should really just fix it so that 64 bit division works in Rust.</p>",
        "id": 626827161,
        "sender_full_name": "Alice Ryhl",
        "timestamp": 1790324925
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What is the preferred approach for the fix?</p>",
        "id": 626827738,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790325043
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can we define <code>__aeabi_uldivmod</code> in compiler builtins?</p>",
        "id": 626836393,
        "sender_full_name": "Alice Ryhl",
        "timestamp": 1790327572
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Like we do for core.o, we could redirect __aeabi_uldivmod and __aeabi_ldivmod in all Rust objects to Rust-only functions, and implement them in asm.</p>",
        "id": 626854813,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790333246
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We already have a solution, but we only apply it for core?</p>",
        "id": 626858957,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790334640
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Let me try to see if I can fix that.</p>",
        "id": 626860754,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790335227
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not quite. The current __rust__aeabi_uldivmod just panics, so if we apply the redirect to all Rust objects, it links but panics at runtime. We need to implement __rust__aeabi_uldivmod and __rust__aeabi_ldivmod in asm.</p>",
        "id": 626862552,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790335831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh</p>",
        "id": 626862988,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790335957
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't it better to get a build error than add a hidden runtime panic?</p>",
        "id": 626863129,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790336002
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why did we decide for this to just panic <span aria-label=\"laughter tears\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f602\" role=\"img\" title=\"laughter tears\">:laughter_tears:</span>  I'm sure there is an interesting discussion behind.</p>",
        "id": 626863609,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790336154
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Was it some kind of hack to be able to build core, ie. it is impossible to build core without 64 bit division or something?</p>",
        "id": 626863791,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790336209
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I meant to have it perform a division, rather than panic</p>",
        "id": 626864167,
        "sender_full_name": "Alice Ryhl",
        "timestamp": 1790336324
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"498029\">Andreas Hindborg</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/291565-Help/topic/64bit.20division.20in.20.60pwm_th1520.2Ers.60/near/626863791\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Was it some kind of hack to be able to build core, ie. it is impossible to build core without 64 bit division or something?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. core.o itself builds, but vmlinux fails to link: core.o references __aeabi_uldivmod, and all core.o symbols are exported, so the linker can't drop them.</p>",
        "id": 626865636,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790336790
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>At any rate, <code>pwm_th1520.ko</code> should have been gated behind <code>CONFIG_64BIT</code>, because it is breaking the arm32 defconfig build.</p>",
        "id": 626866126,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790336950
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Whenever we get 64bit division on 32bit arm working, the driver can drop the gating.</p>",
        "id": 626866217,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790336975
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Some kind of bot surely must have found this out.</p>",
        "id": 626866385,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790337022
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://lore.kernel.org/all/72feff7d-a305-4ca3-9c28-9040c791d3c5@gmail.com/\">https://lore.kernel.org/all/72feff7d-a305-4ca3-9c28-9040c791d3c5@gmail.com/</a><br>\nChristian suggested implementing it in asm before. I think we could do it like this.</p>",
        "id": 626867326,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790337328
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>On arm32, just printing a u64 value can crash the kernel:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">u64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1000</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"n\">pr_info</span><span class=\"o\">!</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"{}</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Internal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">error</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Oops</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">undefined</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">instruction</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">[#</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SMP</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ARM</span>\n<span class=\"n\">PC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">__rust__adddf3</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mh\">0x0</span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"mh\">0x4</span>\n<span class=\"n\">LR</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_RNvMsk_NtNtNtCshy5ABSPeoCG_4core3fmt3num3impy4__fmt</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mh\">0x54</span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"mh\">0x174</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 626873371,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790338993
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that this driver and architecture aren’t the only ones having issues with 64-bit division, 32-bit PowerPC is also affected.</p>\n<p>In c93c194e4ee71a734986fd99a8b1ec9fbb7a9b8b I had to make the <code>__udivdi3</code> and <code>__umoddi3</code> symbols panic at runtime in order to be able to build core, due to formatting of <code>[iu]64</code>, <code>[iu]128</code> and <code>core::time:Duration</code> all making use of 64-bit division.  The <a href=\"http://drm_panic_qr.rs\">drm_panic_qr.rs</a> driver also uses 64-bit division when compiled with <code>CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE=y</code>.</p>\n<p>Back then, I remember the discussions pointing to the idea to use different algorithms in the <code>core</code> crate, but nobody did step forward to implement that. The reasoning was that 64-bit division emulation is way too costly on 32-bit to be hidden behind a seemingly efficient <code>/</code> operator.</p>",
        "id": 626901806,
        "sender_full_name": "Link Mauve",
        "timestamp": 1790345488
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, thanks for clarifying. I think having unavoidable panics hiding in the <code>/</code> operators maybe worse than a slow division algorithm?</p>",
        "id": 627351597,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790584013
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>At least for <code>Duration</code> we have a fallback on 32 bit systems now:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">impl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ops</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">Div</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Delta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Output</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"cp\">#[inline]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">div</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rhs</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Self</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Self</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">Output</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"cp\">#[cfg(CONFIG_64BIT)]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rhs</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">value</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"cp\">#[cfg(not(CONFIG_64BIT))]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\">// SAFETY: This function is always safe to call regardless of the input values</span>\n<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"k\">unsafe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bindings</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">div64_s64</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rhs</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 627351898,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790584097
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could we implement the division symbols in the same way instead of using inline assembly?</p>",
        "id": 627352042,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790584138
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think ideally we'd want to implement <code>__aeabi_uldivmod</code> by calling <code>div64_s64</code> or a similar symbol.</p>",
        "id": 627353768,
        "sender_full_name": "Alice Ryhl",
        "timestamp": 1790584594
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess we would implement <code>uldivmod</code> with <code>div_s64_rem</code> to get the remainder as well?</p>",
        "id": 627369867,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790586609
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or the unsigned version of that one I guess.</p>",
        "id": 627370149,
        "sender_full_name": "Andreas Hindborg",
        "timestamp": 1790586665
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>right, whichever method is the right one</p>",
        "id": 627372936,
        "sender_full_name": "Alice Ryhl",
        "timestamp": 1790587175
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>pwm_th1520 is only supported by 64bit RISC-V so I think that (COMPILE_TEST &amp;&amp; 64BIT) instead of COMPILE_TEST should be used.<br>\nI think that we need to implement __rust__aeabi_uldivmod and __rust__aeabi_ldivmod because the core can  use 64bit div. I'll send patches.</p>",
        "id": 627676398,
        "sender_full_name": "FUJITA Tomonori",
        "timestamp": 1790667372
    }
]